// doc -- a whole idml document, as declarations.
//
// The parser already covered expressions, scripts and UI items; this is the
// outermost layer, and it is what makes a `.idml` file loadable rather than
// merely lexable. A document is a sequence of top-level declarations:
//
//     import "./art.idml"
//     sprite bird from "art/bird.png" grid (1, 4) cell (17, 12)
//     game flappy { display (288, 512) "flappy"  fps 60  start play }
//     scene play { clear 0x70C5CE  entity bird { pos (144, 200) … } }
//     template Pipe { … }
//
// **Sprites and models are references to files, not data.** idml is a layout and
// world language (ARCHITECTURE.md §7.6); the pixels live in a PNG and the engine
// decodes it. `sprite NAME from "path"` binds a name to a file, which is the one
// thing idml still has to say about art.
//
// Every declaration has the same shape -- a keyword, a name, optional arguments,
// an optional braced body -- so there is one parser for all of them and the
// keyword only decides the node kind.

par_doc(int[] pos) {
  int[] kids = [];
  par_dfill(pos, kids);
} return int ast_new_kids(ast_k_file(), "", kids);

par_dfill(int[] pos, int[] kids) {
  while (par_kind(pos) != lex_k_eof()) {
    push(kids, par_top(pos));
  }
} return void;
